    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Aluminium fencing has become a popular choice for front gardens, pool surrounds and garden boundaries. It gives the slim, elegant look of traditional wrought iron, it does not rust, and it needs far less upkeep than timber. When you start planning one, though, the first question is almost always what it will cost.</p>
<p>Prices for an aluminium fence (or "aluminum fence", as it is known in the US) vary by region, supplier and season, so rather than quote figures that will soon be out of date, this guide explains what makes one aluminium fence cost more than another. Once you know the drivers, you can read quotes with confidence and decide where the money is best spent.</p>

<h2 id="material-grade">1. Material grade</h2>
<p>The grade of an aluminium fence describes the wall thickness of the metal and the size of the pickets, rails and posts. It is the biggest single factor in the base material cost, and it also decides how well the fence stands up to knocks and leaning.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Residential grade:</strong> the most common choice for gardens and many pool enclosures. It is light, quick to install and usually the most affordable.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial grade:</strong> thicker walls and larger pickets give more strength and a more substantial look. It costs noticeably more, but it suits busy areas, larger dogs and boundaries where people may lean or climb.</li>
<li><strong>Industrial grade:</strong> the heaviest and most expensive, designed for security and impact resistance. It is rarely needed for a home, though some large estates use it on exposed perimeters.</li>
</ul>
<p>Choose the grade for the job the fence actually does. A decorative border around a front lawn does not need the same strength as a fence holding in a large, determined dog.</p>

<h2 id="height">2. Height</h2>
<p>Height adds cost in a fairly direct way: a taller fence uses more aluminium in every panel and post. Common heights are about 1.2 m (4 ft), 1.5 m (5 ft) and 1.8 m (6 ft), with other heights available to order.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>About 1.2 m (4 ft):</strong> often used for decorative front boundaries and usually the most affordable.</li>
<li><strong>About 1.5 m (5 ft):</strong> a popular middle ground with more presence without feeling enclosed.</li>
<li><strong>About 1.8 m (6 ft):</strong> for back gardens and boundaries where you want a stronger deterrent.</li>
</ul>
<p>Taller fences may also need longer posts set in deeper footings, which adds a little labour. Height is where the rules come in too. In England, a fence over 2 m high, or over 1 m next to a highway used by vehicles, usually needs planning permission, so a tall front fence by a road needs checking first. For a pool enclosure, do not rely on a standard height: check local pool-barrier rules, which can also cover gaps, gates and self-closing latches.</p>

<h2 id="style">3. Style and design</h2>
<p>Aluminium fencing comes in many designs, and the more involved the design, the more it costs to make.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Flat-top:</strong> a horizontal top rail covers the ends of the pickets. It is usually the most economical design and a safe, clean choice near play areas.</li>
<li><strong>Spear-top with finials:</strong> pickets run past the top rail and end in decorative points or finials. It takes more material and more detailed manufacturing, so it costs a little more. Pointed tops are best avoided next to play areas or anywhere children might climb.</li>
<li><strong>Pet-safe lower pickets:</strong> extra short pickets between the main ones narrow the gaps along the bottom of the fence so small pets cannot squeeze through. They add to the material cost for every panel that has them.</li>
<li><strong>Privacy slat panels:</strong> most aluminium fences are open-picket, but some systems offer horizontal slat or solid panels. These use far more metal and are among the most expensive aluminium options.</li>
</ul>

<!--figure:ornate-black-gold-metal-fence|Decorative styles with scrolls, rings or finials cost more per metre than plain flat-top panels.-->

<h2 id="colour-and-finish">4. Colour and finish</h2>
<p>Aluminium fencing is almost always powder-coated: a dry powder is applied and baked on to form a hard finish that resists scratching, fading and peeling. Black is the most common colour and usually the most affordable, because it is made in large volumes; bronze and white are often priced similarly. Less common shades, such as dark green or sand, may be special-order colours that cost more and come with longer lead times. If you want a fence to match window frames or garden furniture, ask early whether that colour is standard or special-order.</p>

<!--figure:black-fence-spear-finials-garden|Black powder coat is the standard finish; other colours and textured coats usually cost extra.-->

<h2 id="labour">5. Labour</h2>
<p>Installation labour is a large part of any fencing job, and how much depends on local rates and the site. Many aluminium systems use pre-made panels that bolt or slot into posts, which helps, but several things add time:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Layout:</strong> a straight, level run is quick. Corners, curves, short sections and panels cut to fit all take longer.</li>
<li><strong>Soil:</strong> rocky ground, heavy clay or old tree roots slow down post-hole digging and may need a power auger or breaker. Some installers price difficult digging separately, so ask how they handle it.</li>
<li><strong>Access:</strong> if materials, concrete and spoil have to be carried through the house or a narrow side gate, the job takes longer.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before any digging, check for buried cables and pipes: use utility plans or a cable avoidance tool in the UK, and call 811 in the US.</p>

<h2 id="gates">6. Gates</h2>
<p>Gates are the hardest-working part of a fence and one of the most expensive per metre. You cannot price a fence by length alone without counting them separately.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pedestrian gates:</strong> single gates for a path or side passage. Each one needs stronger posts, hinges and a latch, and self-closing hinges and latches for a pool gate add a little more.</li>
<li><strong>Driveway gates:</strong> much wider single or double gates that need heavier posts and hardware to stop them sagging. They cost far more than a pedestrian gate.</li>
<li><strong>Automation:</strong> an electric opener adds the motor, controls, wiring and specialist labour, and it is one of the biggest single extras. An automated gate must be installed by a specialist and include safety features such as obstacle detection, and you will need an electrician for the power supply.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="site-preparation">7. Site preparation</h2>
<p>Before the first post goes in, the site needs to be ready, and this is an often-overlooked cost.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Old fence removal:</strong> taking down an existing timber or chain-link fence, digging out old footings and disposing of the waste all add labour and possibly a skip.</li>
<li><strong>Clearing:</strong> overgrown shrubs or small trees along the fence line may need clearing so installers can work. Take care around trees, and check for tree preservation orders in the UK.</li>
<li><strong>Permits, surveys and HOA approval:</strong> rules vary, so check with your local planning authority or building department, and with any homeowners' association (HOA) in the US, which may set rules on height, style and colour. If your boundary is unclear, a land surveyor can confirm it before you build. Ask about any fees when you contact them.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="terrain">8. Terrain and slopes</h2>
<p>How a fence handles a slope affects both the look and the price.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rackable panels:</strong> many aluminium panels are designed to rack, so the pickets stay vertical while the rails follow the slope. On a gentle, even slope this gives a smooth line with no gaps underneath and usually adds little cost.</li>
<li><strong>Stepped panels:</strong> on steeper ground, each panel is set level and the fence steps down the hill. This needs longer posts and more precise setting, and it leaves triangular gaps at the base that may need filling for pets, so it costs more.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check the rackable range of the system you are quoted for. A slope beyond it means stepping, or special panels, which changes the price.</p>

<!--figure:metal-fence-following-curved-path|Curves and slopes add cost, because panels must be racked or stepped to follow the ground.-->

<h2 id="aluminium-vs-other-materials">9. How aluminium compares with other fencing</h2>
<p>An aluminium fence usually costs more to buy than chain-link or basic timber, but it is often more affordable than wrought iron. The difference narrows over time because aluminium needs no staining, painting or replacing of rotten sections, only a periodic wash and a check of the coating. For many homeowners, that low upkeep is what makes it good value in the long run.</p>

<h2 id="comparing-quotes">10. What an itemised quote should include</h2>
<p>Give two or three installers the same measurements and specification, and ask each for an itemised quote covering:</p>
<ul>
<li>The total length, height and number of panels, with any racked or stepped sections marked.</li>
<li>The grade, including picket, rail and post sizes, and the manufacturer's system name.</li>
<li>The style: flat-top or spear-top, any pet-safe lower pickets and any privacy slat panels.</li>
<li>The colour, and whether it is standard or special-order, with the lead time.</li>
<li>How posts are set, including footing depth and concrete.</li>
<li>Each gate, with its width, hinges, latch and any self-closing hardware; for an automated gate, the opener, safety features and who does the electrical work.</li>
<li>Removal and disposal of the old fence, clearing and any extra charge for difficult digging.</li>
<li>Any permits, approvals or surveys, and who arranges them.</li>
<li>The workmanship guarantee and the manufacturer's warranty on panels and finish.</li>
</ul>
<p>If one quote is much lower than the rest, check which of these items it leaves out or downgrades.</p>

<h2 id="ways-to-save">11. Ways to keep the cost down</h2>
<ul>
<li>Choose the lowest grade that suits the job, and keep heavier grades for the stretches that need them.</li>
<li>Pick a standard colour and a flat-top design.</li>
<li>Keep the height to what you need; a lower front fence with planting behind it can feel generous.</li>
<li>Limit gates, and think carefully before automating a driveway gate.</li>
<li>Keep the layout simple, with fewer corners and cut panels.</li>
<li>Remove an old fence or clear planting yourself, if the installer agrees and you can dispose of the waste properly.</li>
<li>Book outside the busiest season, when installers may have more flexibility.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask for one panel and post in your chosen grade and colour to be priced in the next grade up as well. Seeing the difference on a single section makes it much easier to decide whether the stronger fence is worth it.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Can an aluminium fence rust or corrode?</h3>
<p>Aluminium does not rust the way iron does, but it is not immune to corrosion. Where the powder coating is scratched or chipped, the metal can corrode, usually showing as a white, chalky powder, and salt spray in coastal gardens or from gritted roads speeds this up. Touch up damage promptly with the manufacturer's recommended paint, and use stainless steel or manufacturer-supplied fixings so they do not rust and stain the panels.</p>
<h3>Is an aluminium fence strong enough for a large dog?</h3>
<p>It can be, if you choose the right grade. For large or strong dogs, a heavier grade, such as commercial grade, with thicker pickets and rails is the safer choice, because it resists bending and impact better. Check the gap at the base too, and consider pet-safe lower pickets for smaller dogs.</p>
<h3>How long does installation take?</h3>
<p>For a typical garden, installation is often a few days on site, plus lead time for the materials to arrive, which can be longer for special-order colours or styles. Slopes, rocky ground, gates and automation all add time, so ask your installer for a realistic schedule.</p>
<h3>Can I install an aluminium fence myself?</h3>
<p>Many aluminium systems come as ready-made panels that bolt or slot into posts, so a short, straight run on level ground is a reasonable DIY project. Setting posts accurately in concrete is the hard part, because small errors stop the panels fitting. Slopes, long runs and gates are usually worth leaving to a professional, and automated gates should always be installed by a specialist.</p>
<h3>Does an aluminium fence add value to a home?</h3>
<p>A well-kept aluminium fence can improve kerb appeal and make a garden feel finished and secure, which may appeal to buyers with children or pets. Whether it adds value depends on your local market and the condition of the property as a whole, so treat it mainly as an investment in how you use your outdoor space.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>The cost of an aluminium fence comes down to the grade, height, style and finish, the gates, the labour and the site itself, from old fence removal to slopes and soil. An itemised quote based on the same specification shows you where the money goes. Before you commit, read our guide to common <a href="/blog/aluminium-fence-mistakes">aluminium fence mistakes</a>, and plan the upkeep with our <a href="/blog/aluminium-fence-maintenance-by-season">aluminium fence maintenance by season</a> guide; if you are weighing up a more solid screen, see <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-cost">privacy fence cost</a>. You can also explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your outdoor space with a designer.</p>
